1. What you may do without an agreement

   You may read the Enterprise Software, copy it and change it, but only to
   develop and test it, or to develop and test changes you plan to
   contribute back to Slatebox LLC. You may run it for those purposes on
   your own computers and test systems.

2. What needs an agreement

   Any other use needs a written agreement with Slatebox LLC. "Other use"
   includes, without limit:

   a. running the Enterprise Software, or a changed version of it, in
      production, for real users, or to process real data;
   b. offering it, or a service built on it, to anyone else, hosted or not;
   c. selling, sublicensing, renting or distributing it, or a changed
      version of it, on its own or as part of another product.

   Slatebox LLC runs the Enterprise Software as part of Slatebox Cloud. Using
   Slatebox Cloud under its terms of service is the agreement that covers
   people who use these features there.

   The self-hosted Slatebox images that Slatebox LLC publishes include the
   Enterprise Software, locked. Receiving and running such an image is
   allowed; the Enterprise Software in it stays locked. An on-prem license
   key that Slatebox LLC issues for an install, under the terms it is sold
   with, is the written agreement for running the Enterprise Software in
   production on that install while the key is valid. Removing, changing or
   getting around the license check, or making license keys, is not allowed.

